  "MA202": {
    "code": "MA202",
    "name": "Mathematical Methods for Communication Engineering",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Elements of Probability\nIntroduction to Probability; Sample Space and Events; Probabilities Defined on Events; Conditional\nProbabilities; Independent Events; Total Probability; Bayes’ Formula.",
      "Module 2: Random Variables and Distribution\nRandom Variables: Concept of Random Variables; Distribution and Density Function; Jointly Distributed\nRandom of Variables; Conditional and Joint Density Distribution function; Function of Random Variables;\nExpected Value: Mean, Variance and moments of random variable; Joint moments, conditional expectation;\ncovariance and correlation; Some special distributions: Uniform and Gaussian distributions; Bernoulli,\n\n\nBinomial, and Poisson distributions.",
      "Module 3: Statistics\nThe Sample Mean; The Central Limit Theorem; The Sample Variance; Sampling Distributions from a\nNormal Population; Sampling from a Finite Population; Parameter Estimation; Maximum Likelihood\nEstimators; Interval Estimates; Significance Levels; Tests Concerning the Mean of a Normal Population;\nTesting the Equality of Means of Two Normal Populations; Hypothesis Tests Concerning the Variance of a\nNormal Population.",
      "Module 4: Complex Analysis\nComplex Numbers, geometric representation, powers and roots of complex numbers, Functions of a\ncomplex variable, Analytic functions, Cauchy-Riemann equations; elementary functions, Conformal\nmapping (for linear transformation); Contours and contour integration, Cauchy’s theorem, Cauchy integral\nformula; Power Series and properties, Taylor series, Laurent series, Zeros, singularities, poles, essential\nsingularities, Residue theorem, Evaluation of real integrals and improper integrals."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. E. Kreyszig, “Advanced engineering mathematics”, 8th Edition, John Wiley (1999).",
      "2. Sheldon M. Ross, “Introduction to Probability and Statistics for Engineers and Scientists”, 5th Edition,\nAcademic Press.",
      "3. Sheldon M. Ross, “Introduction to Probability Models”, 11th Edition, Academic Press.",
      "4. Athanasios Papoulis, and S. Unnikrishna Pillai, “Probability, Random Variables, and Stochastic\nProcesses”, McGraw-Hill, 2002."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "This course is crafted to provide engineers and scientists with a comprehensive grasp of probability,\nrandom variables, statistics and complex analysis. Further, with a focus on key principles such as\nprobability, complex variables and their practical applications, students will develop a deep understanding\nof applied mathematics and its real-world implications."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability"
    ]
  },
  "EC200": {
    "code": "EC200",
    "name": "Electromagnetic",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Vector Calculus (10 hours)\nCoordinate Systems and Transformation, Vector Calculus: Line, Surface, and Volume Integrals, Gradient\nof a Scalar, Divergence of a Vector and Divergence Theorem, Curl of a Vector and Stokes's Theorem,\nLaplacian of a Scalar.",
      "Module 2: Electrostatic Fields (16 hours)\nElectrostatic Fields: Coulomb's Law and Field Intensity, Electric Fields due to Continuous Charge\nDistributions, Electric Flux Density, Gauss's Law, An Electric Dipole, Flux Lines.\nElectric Fields in Material Space: Convection and Conduction Currents, Dielectric Constant and Strength,\nContinuity Equation and Relaxation Time, Boundary Conditions, Electrostatic Boundary- Value Problems:\nPoisson's and Laplace's Equations, Uniqueness Theorem.",
      "Module 3: Magnetostatic Fields (12 hours)\nBiot-Savart's Law, Ampere's Circuit Law, Magnetic Flux Density, Maxwell's Equations for Static EM\nFields, Magnetic Scalar and Vector Potentials, Magnetic Forces: Forces due to Magnetic Fields, Magnetic\nTorque and Moment, A Magnetic Dipole, Magnetic Boundary Conditions.",
      "Module 4: Wave Propagation and Transmission Lines (18 hours)\nFaraday's Law, Transformer and Motional EMFs, Displacement Current, Maxwell's Equations in Final\nForms, Time-Varying Potentials, Time-Harmonic Fields, Wave Propagation in Lossy Dielectrics, Plane\nWaves in Lossless Dielectrics, Plane Waves in Free Space, Plane Waves in Good Conductors, Skin effect,\nPower and the Poynting Vector, Reflection of a Plane Wave at Normal Incidence, Reflection of a Plane\nWave at Oblique Incidence.\nIntroduction, Transmission Line Parameters, Transmission Line Equations, Input Impedance, SWR, and\nPower."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Matthew N. O. Sadiku, “Principles of Electromagnetics”, 4th Edition, oxford university press, 2010.",
      "2. David k. Cheng, “Field and Wave Electromagnetics”, 2nd Edition, Pearson Education India, 2014.",
      "3. W. H. Hayt, J A Buck and M Jaleel Akthar, “Engineering Electromagnetics”, 8th Edition, McGraw\nHill Education, 2017.",
      "4. David J. Griffith, “Introduction to Electrodynamics”, 4th Edition, Pearson Education India Learning\nPrivate Limited, 2015."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. Understand electromagnetic fields and wave propagation.",
      "2. Explore vector calculus, electrostatic and magneto static fields.",
      "3. Analyze electromagnetic systems.",
      "4. Learn about transmission lines and wave propagation in different media.",
      "5. Apply Maxwell’s Equations and use boundary conditions to solve problems in electromagnetics."
    ],
    "outcomes": []
  },
