  "EC301": {
    "code": "EC301",
    "name": "Digital Signal Processing",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: The Discrete Transforms (8 hours)\nReviews of signals, systems, Fourier series, Fourier transform, and Z transform, DFT, properties, Digital\nfiltering using DFT, linear and circular convolution, overlap add and overlap save, FFT, DIT FFT, DIF\nFFT, other discrete transforms: DCT, DST, Hartley, Hadamard, Hilbert transform.",
      "Module 2: Design of FIR and IIR Filters (10 hours)\nFIR Filter Design: Ideal characteristics of standard filters, types of FIR filter, FIR filter design using\nfrequency sampling method, windowing method, maximally flat FIR filter approximation, FIR filter\napproximation by optimization.\nIIR Filter Design: Analog filter approximations, continuous time to discrete time transformations: impulse\ninvariant technique, bilinear transformation, Butterworth filter, Chebyshev filter, elliptic filter, frequency\ntransformation.",
      "Module 3: Realizations of Digital Filters (8 hours)\nRepresentation of system by linear constant coefficient difference equations, block diagram and signal flow\nrepresentation, Structures for FIR filters: Direct form, lattice form, cascade form, structures for IIR filters:\nDirect form, canonic form, cascade form and parallel form, comparison of different structures.",
      "Module 4: Finite Word Length Effect (8 hours)\nDSP algorithm implementation and finite word length effect: Number representation and overflow,\nQuantization Process and Errors, fixed and floating point numbers, coefficient quantization, A/D\nconversion noise analysis, Low sensitivity digital filters, Limit Cycle oscillations in IIR digital filters.",
      "Module 5: Advanced Topics in DSP (8 hours)\nMultirate Systems: Applications, sampling, the up-sampler, the down-sampler, signal rate-changing and\ninterpolation, half-band filters.\nAdaptive Systems: Motivation of Adaptive filter, Wiener filter, gradient search algorithm, steepest descent\nalgorithm, LMS and Its variants algorithms.\nText/"
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Alan V. Oppenheim and R. W. Schafer, “Discrete-Time Signal Processing”, 3rd Edition. Pearson,",
      "2. John G. Proakis and D. G. Manolakis, “Digital Signal Processing: Principles, Algorithms and\nApplications”, 4th Edition. Pearson, 2007.",
      "3. Sanjit K. Mitra, “Digital Signal Processing: A Computer-Based Approach”, 4th Edition. Tata\nMcGraw-Hill, 2013.",
      "4. P. P. Vaidyanathan, “Multirate Systems and Filter Banks”, Prentice Hall, 1993.",
      "5. B. Widrow and S. D. Stearns, “Adaptive Signal Processing”, Pearson Education, 2002.",
      "6. S. Haykin, “Adaptive Filter Theory”, Pearson Education, 2013.",
      "7. S. Esakkirajan, T. Veerakumar, and B. N. Subudhi, “Digital Signal Processing”, 1st Edition, Tata\nMcGraw Hill Education, 2021."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. This course is structured to provide students with a comprehensive knowledge of digital signal\nprocessing for engineering applications.",
      "2. It highlights crucial concepts such as discrete Fourier Analysis of signals and systems for delving into\ntheir practical applications.",
      "3. The course introduces the structures of systems, filter design techniques, special filters and\ntransforms, and multi-rate systems.",
      "4. It is anticipated that students will develop a deep understanding of representation of digital signals\nand systems, processing methods, and their real-world applications."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability"
    ]
  },
