  "EC501": {
    "code": "EC501",
    "name": "Biomedical Signal Processing",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Introduction to Human Body and Biomedical Signals (10 hours)\nBioelectricity: Resting, action and graded potentials, Nernst and GHK equation. Basics of muscular,\ncardiovascular and nervous system. ECG, PCG, EMG and EEG signals, their origin, characteristics and\napplications in medical diagnosis.",
      "Module 2: Review of Digital Signal Processing (8 hours)\nFFT and STFT, periodogram, issues of leakage, resolution, bias and variance in periodograms and related\nsolutions. Bias and normalization in Welch's method, multitaper method, Blackman-Tukey spectral\nestimator, Daniell's spectral estimator, measures derived from PSD, cross-spectral density function,\ntransfer function estimate, coherence function.\nPSD analysis of PCG signals, EEG signals, and speech signals.",
      "Module 3: Signal Preprocessing and Filtering (10 hours)\nSignal normalization, segmentation, delineation and epoching, time-domain filtering: synchronized\naveraging, moving average, integration, and derivative-based filters, frequency-domain filtering with notch\nfilter, optimal filtering: the Wiener Filter, adaptive filtering: Newton’s method, steepest descent algorithm,\nLMS adaptation algorithm and its variants, recursive least-squares (RLS) algorithms. Applications in utility\nnoise removal, fetus ECG separation from mother ECG signals etc.",
      "Module 4: Event Detection (6 hours)\nRepresentative cases of events (viz. P, QRS and T wave in ECG), Classification of QRS detection methods,\nderivative based approaches for QRS detection: Pan Tompkins algorithm.",
      "Module 5: Modelling of Biomedical Systems (8 hours)\nParametric system modelling, Autoregressive model, Autocorrelation method, Application to biomedical\nsignals like EEG signals, Computation of model parameters, Levinson-Durbin algorithm, Computation of\ngain factor, Covariance method, Spectral matching and parameterization, Model order selection, Relation\nbetween AR and cepstral coefficients."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. R. M. Rangayyan, “Biomedical Signal Analysis: A case Based Approach”, IEEE Press, John Wiley\n& Sons. Inc, 2002.",
      "2. Willis J. Tompkins, “Biomedical Digital Signal Processing”, EEE, PHI, 2004.",
      "3. D. C. Reddy, “Biomedical Signal Processing: Principles and Techniques”, Tata McGraw-Hill\nPublishing Co. Ltd, 2005.",
      "4. J. G. Webster, “Medical Instrumentation: Application & Design”, John Wiley & Sons Inc., 2001.",
      "5. C. Raja Rao and S. K. Guha, “Principles of Medical Electronics and Biomedical Instrumentation”,\nUniversities Press, 2001.",
      "6. A.V. Oppenheim and R. W, Shafer, “Discrete-time Signal Processing”, Prentice Hall, Englewood\nCliffs, NJ, 1989.",
      "7. Steven M. Kay, “Modern spectral estimation theory and application”, Prentice Hall, Englewood\nCliffs, NJ, 1988."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. To learn biological and engineering principles related to the human body.",
      "2. To understand the origin and role of bio-electricity, nature of electrophysiological and other\nbiomedical signals.",
      "3. To apply various statistical and signal processing techniques for modelling, and processing\nbiomedical signals in various healthcare applications.",
      "4. To develop skills to integrate the knowledge of anatomy and physiology, biomedical signals,\nstatistical and signal processing methods, and modeling for solving real-world medical applications."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability:"
    ]
  },
