  "EC510": {
    "code": "EC510",
    "name": "Wireless Communication",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Principles of Wireless Communications (8 hours)\nIntroduction to 2G/3G/4G/5G wireless communications, modelling of wireless systems, Rayleigh fading\nwireless channel, BER performance, channel estimation for single antenna systems, introduction to\ndiversity, multiple receive antenna system model and its BER performance, realization of independent\npaths, diversity system model, maximal ratio combining, channel estimation for multi-antenna system,\ndiversity order.",
      "Module 2: Wireless-System Planning (7 hours)\nLarge scale fading, free space propagation model, ground reflection model, Okumura and Hata models, log\nnormal shadowing, receiver noise analysis, practical link budget analysis, teletraffic system model and\nsteady-state analysis, determination of percentage of coverage area.",
      "Module 3: Small Scale Fading and Multipath Propagation (7 hours)\nImpulse response model of a multipath channel, parameters of mobile multipath channels: time dispersion\nparameters, coherence bandwidth, ISI and coherence bandwidth, Doppler spread and coherence time, types\nof small scale fading: flat fading, frequency selective fading, fast fading, slow fading, Jakes model for\nwireless channel correlation.",
      "Module 4: GSM system (FDM-TDMA) (6 hours)\nDescription of GSM system block diagram, SIR analysis, channel reuse analysis: D/R ratio, N_reuse, cell\nsectorization, spectrum efficiency, channel allocation and multicell Erlang models, call blocking analysis,\nhandovers – techniques, models and analysis.",
      "Module 5: Fundamental Concepts of Spread Spectrum Systems (6 hours)\nCode Division for Multiple Access (CDMA), Pseudo noise sequence, performance of direct sequence\nspread spectrum systems, analysis of direct sequence spread spectrum systems, the processing gain and\nanti-jamming margin, frequency hopped spread spectrum systems, time hopped spread spectrum systems,\nsynchronization of spread spectrum systems, RAKE receiver.",
      "Module 6: Multiple-Input Multiple-Output Wireless Communications (8 hours)\nMultiple-Input Multiple-Output Wireless Communications: MIMO system model, zero-forcing receiver,\nMMSE receiver, SVD and MIMO capacity, Alamouti codes, OSTBC, V-BLAST, OFDM, MIMO-OFDM,\nSC-FDMA receiver."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Aditya K. Jagannatham, “Principles of Modern Wireless Communication Systems, Theory and\nPractice”, 1st Edition, McGraw Hill Education, 2017.",
      "2. Theodore Rappaport, “Wireless Communications: Principles and Practice”, 2nd Edition, Pearson,",
      "3. Anurag Kumar, D. Manjunath, and Joy Kuri, “Wireless Networking”, Morgan Kaufmann Publishers,",
      "4. Simon Haykin and Michael Moher, “Modern Wireless Communication”, Prentice Hall, 2005.",
      "5. Andrea Goldsmith, “Wireless Communications”, Cambridge University Press, 2005."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. This course aims to cover the fundamental concepts and insights behind the development of modern\nwireless communication technologies such as 2G/3G/4G/5G.",
      "2. Students will be exposed to modeling and analysis of wireless system along with several key\ntechnologies of the modern communication such as GSM, CDMA, SIMO/ MISO, OFDM, MIMO,\nMIMO-OFDM in detail.",
      "3. It is anticipated that students will develop a deep understanding of nature and modeling of wireless\nmediums, performances of various modulation schemes, large and small-scale signal fading,\ndiversity, channel estimation, signal reception techniques, multiple access technologies, teletraffic\ntheory."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability:"
    ]
  },
