  "EC526": {
    "code": "EC526",
    "name": "Computational Electromagnetics",
    "branch": "ECE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/ECE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "ECE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 1,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Analytical Computational Electromagnetics (12 hours)\nComputational electromagnetics: research trends and applications, analytical methods: method of\nseparation of variables, orthogonal expansion, Eigen function expansion, numerical integration, numerical\nquadrature and orthogonal polynomials, stationary phase method for evaluation of radiation integrals.",
      "Module 2: Electromagnetic scattering (10 hours)\nMaxwell’ equations, source representation, Green’s function, source-field relationship, electric field\nintegral equations (EFIE), magnetic field integral equations (MFIE), Analytical solutions for TM and TE\nscattering of PEC and Dielectric cylinders, subdomain basis functions, entire domain basis functions,\n\n\ndiscretization errors.",
      "Module 3: Method of Moments and FDM (10 hours)\nThe method of weighted residuals (Method of Moments), TM wave scattering by a conducting cylinder,\nfinite difference method (FDM), interface and boundary conditions, absorbing boundary conditions,\napplication of FDM in the analysis of parallel plate capacitor.",
      "Module 4: FEM and FDTD (10 hours)\nFinite element method (FEM), Calculation of capacitance of parallel plate, Introduction to Finite Difference\nTime Domain (FDTD) method, Applications of FDTD in reflection of a wave at an interface, determination\nof propagation constant in the medium."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. A. F. Peterson, S. L. Ray, and R. Mittra, Computational Methods for Electromagnetics, Universities\nPress, 2001.",
      "2. Ramesh Garg, Analytical and Computational methods in Electromagnetics, Artech House, 2008.",
      "3. Jian-ming Jin, Theory and computation of electromagnetic fields, Wiley-IEEE press, 2015.\n\n\nList of Open Electives (OEs) (Offered for Other than ECE)\nS. No. Course Code Course Name L T P Credits"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. Introduce fundamental analytical and computational techniques in electromagnetics.",
      "2. Develop problem-solving skills using analytical methods like separation of variables and\neigenfunction expansion.",
      "3. Explore electromagnetic scattering and integral equation formulations.",
      "4. Understand numerical techniques such as MoM, FDM, FEM, and FDTD.",
      "5. Apply computational methods to real-world electromagnetic problems."
    ],
    "outcomes": []
  },
