  "CS253": {
    "code": "CS253",
    "name": "Theory of Computation",
    "branch": "CSE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/CSE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "CSE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Introduction to Automata: Introduction to formal proof, additional forms of proof, inductive\nproofs, finite automata (FA), deterministic finite automata (DFA), non-deterministic finite\nautomata (NFA), Finite Automata with Epsilon transitions.\nRegular expression and languages: Introduction to regular expression, building regular\nexpression, converting DFA to a regular expression, converting regular expression to DFA,",
      "pumping lemma and its applications to prove languages not to be regular, closure properties of\nregular languages, minimization of automata.\nContext free grammars (CFG) and languages: Definition, derivations, parse trees, ambiguity in\ngrammars and languages, pushdown automata (PDA): Definition, Graphical notation,\ndeterministic and nondeterministic, instantaneous descriptions of PDAs, language acceptance\nby final states and by empty stack, equivalence of the CFG and PDAs, pumping lemma for\nCFLs, closure properties of CFLs, decision problems for CFLs.\nComputability Theory: Introduction to Turing machines, language acceptance by Turing\nmachines, Turing machine transition diagrams, Church-Turing hypothesis, Chomsky\nhierarchy, recursively enumerable sets, existence of non-recursively enumerable notion of\nundecidable problems, universality of Turing machine, separation of recursive and recursively\nenumerable classes, notion of reduction, undecidable problems of Turing machines.\nComplexity Theory: Notion of tractability/feasibility, the classes NP and co-NP, polynomial\ntime many-one reduction, completeness under this reduction, NP-completeness of\npropositional satisfiability, other variants of satisfiability, NP-complete problems from other\ndomains: graphs (clique, vertex cover, independent sets, Hamiltonian cycle), number problem\n(partition), set cover."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. J.E. Hopcroft and J.D. Ullman. “Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages of\nComputations”, Addison-Wesley, 2008.",
      "2. Michael Sipser, “Introduction to the Theory of Computation”, Books/Cole Thomson Learning,",
      "3. H. R. Lewis and C. Papadimitriou, “Elements of Theory of Computation”, Prentice-Hall, 2015.",
      "4. D. C. Kozen, Automata and Computability, Addison Wesley, 1994.",
      "5. J. C. Martin, Introduction to Languages and the Theory of Computation, McGraw Hill, 2002"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "This course introduces models of computation: Regular languages models, context-free\nlanguages models. Also, this course emphasizes computability and computational\ncomplexity theory. Topics include decidable and undecidable problems, reducibility,\n\nrecursive function theory, completeness, hierarchy theorems, inherently complex\nproblems, and interactive proof systems."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability to"
    ]
  },
