  "ME253": {
    "code": "ME253",
    "name": "Machine Dynamics and Vibrations",
    "branch": "MCE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/MCE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "MCE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-1-0",
    "credits": 4,
    "modules": [
      "Flywheels and Governors: Turning moment diagram, Fluctuation of energy and speed,\ncoefficient of fluctuation of speed, use of crank effort diagram, calculation of flywheel size;\nAdvantages of governors, Analysis of different types of governors, effect of sleeve friction,\ncharacteristic of governors, controlling forces curves, sensitivity, hunting phenomena in\ngovernors, stability, governor effort and power.\nBalancing: Balancing of rotating masses in single plane and in different parallel planes,\nbalancing of slider crank mechanisms, balancing of in-line, V- and locomotive engines.",
      "Friction Devices: Advantages and disadvantages of belt drives system, belt drive system,\nfriction in pivots and collars, power screws, plate and cone clutches, band and block brakes.\nGyroscope: Motion of rigid body in 3- dimensions, Angular momentum, Gyroscopic action,\nequation for regular precession and gyroscopic torque, applications of gyroscope.\nMechanical Vibrations: Basic terminology related to vibrations; Conservative systems; Free\nvibrations of systems without and with damping; Equilibrium and energy methods for\ndetermining natural frequency of vibratory system; Rayleigh’s method, Free vibrations of\nsystem with viscous damping, over damped, critically and under damped systems, logarithmic\ndecrement; Forced vibrations of systems with viscous damping, equivalent viscous damping;\nImpressed forces due to unbalanced masses and excitation of supports, vibration isolation,\ntransmissibility, whirling of shaft; Introduction to multi degree of freedom system vibrations:\nDiscrete and continuous systems, Numerical methods"
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "/Text Material:",
      "1. R. L. Norton, \"Design of Machinery – An Introduction to the Synthesis and Analysis\nof Mechanisms and Machines,\" 6th Ed, McGraw Hill, 2020.",
      "2. S. S. Rattan, \"Theory of Machines,\" 4th Ed, McGraw Hill, 2017.",
      "3. S. S. Rao, \"Mechanical Vibrations,\" 6th Ed, Pearson Education, 2018.\nA. K. Grover, \"Mechanical Vibrations,\" 8th Ed, Nem Chand & Bros, 2009.",
      "4. L. Meirovitch, \"Elements of Vibration Analysis,\" 2nd Ed, McGraw Hill, 1975.",
      "5. N. Sclater and N. P. Chironis, \"Mechanisms and Mechanical Devices Sourcebook,\" 5th\nEd, McGraw Hill, 2020.",
      "6. S. Singh, \"Theory of Machines,\" 3rd Ed, Pearson Education, 2012.",
      "7. W. T. Thomson, \"Theory of Vibration with Applications,\" 3rd Ed., CBS Publishers and\nDistributors, 2003."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "The aim of this course is to equip students with a profound understanding of dynamic aspects\nin machinery. Covering flywheels and governors for energy and speed control, balancing of\nrotating masses, friction devices like belt drives, and essential concepts of gyroscopes and\nmechanical vibrations, the course prepares students to analyze and design machines with\nconsideration for dynamic forces, balancing, and vibration control."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability to -"
    ]
  },
