  "ME505": {
    "code": "ME505",
    "name": "Dynamics of Mechanical Systems",
    "branch": "MCE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/MCE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "MCE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Basic concepts: Inertial coordinate system, fundamental laws of motion, mechanics of particles\nand system of particles, principles of linear and angular momentum, work-energy principles.\nLagrangian dynamics: Degrees of freedom, generalized coordinates and generalized forces,\nholonomic and non-holonomic constraints, Lagrange’s equation from d’Alembert’s principles,",
      "application of Lagrange’s equation for conservative and non-conservative autonomous systems\nwith holonomic and non-holonomic constraints, applications to systems with very small\ndisplacements and impulsive motion; Hamilton principle from d’Alembert’s principle,\nLagrange equation from Hamilton’s principle.\nMulti-body dynamics: Space and fixed body coordinate systems, coordinate transformation\nmatrix, direction cosines, Euler angles, Euler parameters, finite and infinitesimal rotations, time\nderivatives of transformations matrices, angular velocity and acceleration vectors, equations of\nmotion of multi-body system, Newton-Euler equations, planer kinematic and dynamic analysis,\nkinematic revolute joints, joint reaction forces, simple applications of planer systems.\nStability of motion: Fundamental concept in stability, autonomous systems and phase plane\nplots, Routh’s criteria for stability, Liapunov’s method, Liapunov’s stability theorems,\nLiapunov’s function to determine stability of the system.\nControl system dynamics: Open and close loop systems, block diagrams, transfer functions\nand characteristics equations, proportional integral and derivative control actions and their\ncharacteristics."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "/Text Material:",
      "1. J. H. Ginsberg, \"Advanced Engineering Dynamics,\" Harper and Row, 1988.",
      "2. L. Meirovitch, \"Methods of Analytical Dynamics,\" McGraw Hill Inc., 1970.",
      "3. H. Josephs and R. Huston, \"Dynamics of Mechanical Systems,\" CRC Press, 2002.",
      "4. K. Ogata, \"System Dynamics,\" 4th Ed., Prentice Hall, 2003.",
      "5. R. L. Woods and K. L. Lawrence, \"Modeling and Simulation of Dynamic Systems,\"\nPrentice Hall, 1997.",
      "6. R. S. Esfandiari and B. Lu, \"Modeling and Analysis of Dynamic Systems,\" CRC Press,",
      "7. A. C. Karnopp, D. L. Margolis, and R. C. Rosenberg, \"System Dynamics: Modeling\nand Simulation of Mechatronic Systems,\" 4th Ed., Wiley, 2006.",
      "8. R. A. Layton, \"Principles of Analytical System Dynamics (Mechanical Engineering\nSeries),\" Springer.",
      "9. M. Geradin and D. J. Rixen, \"Mechanical Vibrations: Theory and Application to\nStructural Dynamics,\" 3rd Ed, Wiley, 2015.",
      "10. C. T. F. Ross, \"Dynamics of Mechanical Systems,\" Wiley, 1997.",
      "11. N. J. Kasdin and D. A. Paley, \"Engineering Dynamics – A Comprehensive\nIntroduction,\" Princeton University Press, 2011.",
      "12. A. Ghosh and A. K. Mallik, “Theory of Mechanics and Machines,” East West Pub.,"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "The aim of this course is to furnish students with a comprehensive understanding of mechanical\nsystem dynamics. Covering fundamental laws of motion, Lagrangian dynamics, multi-body\ndynamics, stability analysis, and control system dynamics, the course enables students to\nanalyze and model the behavior of mechanical systems. Emphasizing key concepts like\ngeneralized coordinates, Euler angles, and stability criteria, students gain the skills to address\ncomplex dynamic problems in mechanical engineering."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability to -"
    ]
  },
  "ME506": {
    "code": "ME506",
    "name": "Fatigue in Design",
    "branch": "MCE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/MCE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "MCE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Introduction: Mechanical failure modes, importance of fatigue considerations in design,\nstrategies in fatigue design, fatigue design criteria, fatigue fracture surfaces and macroscopic\nfeatures, fatigue mechanisms.\nFatigue Tests and Stress-Life Approach: Fatigue loading, stress-life (S-N) curves, mean\nstress effects on S-N behavior, factors influencing S-N behavior, notches and their effects.\nCyclic Deformation and Strain-Life Approach: Monotonic and cyclic stress-strain behavior,\nstrain-life curve, factors influencing strain-life behavior.\nFatigue from Variable Amplitude Loading: Variable amplitude loading, linear and nonlinear\ndamage theories, cycle counting, crack propagation under variable amplitude loading.\nEnvironmental Effects: Corrosion fatigue, fretting fatigue, low-temperature fatigue, high-\ntemperature fatigue."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "/Text Material:",
      "1. R. I. Stephens, A. Fatemi, R. R. Stephens, and H. O. Fuchs, \"Metal Fatigue in\nEngineering,\" John Wiley & Sons, 2000.",
      "2. J. A. Bannantine, J. J. Comer, and J. L. Handrock, \"Fundamentals of Metal Fatigue\nAnalysis,\" Prentice Hall, Upper Saddle River, NJ, 1990.",
      "3. J. A. Collins, \"Failure of Materials in Mechanical Design: Analysis, Prediction,\nPrevention,\" John Wiley & Sons, 1993.",
      "4. S. Suresh, \"Fatigue of Materials,\" Cambridge University Press, 1998."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "The aim of the course is to emphasize the importance of fatigue considerations in design and\nto explore strategies for fatigue design"
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "At the completion of this course, the student shall acquire knowledge and ability to -"
    ]
  },
