  "CV539": {
    "code": "CV539",
    "name": "Computational Fluid Dynamics",
    "branch": "CVE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/CVE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "CVE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1 Introduction: Classification and Overview of Numerical Methods: Conservation\nequation; mass; momentum and energy equations; convective forms of the equations and\ngeneral description. Classification into various types of equations, parabolic elliptic and\nhyperbolic; boundary and initial conditions; over view of numerical methods.",
      "Module 2 Finite Difference Technique and Finite Volume Technique: Finite difference\nmethods; different means for formulating finite difference equation; Taylor series expansion,\nintegration over element, local function method; treatment of boundary conditions; boundary\nlayer treatment; variable property; interface and free surface treatment; accuracy of finite\ndifference method.\nFinite volume methods; different types of finite volume grids; approximation of surface and\nvolume integrals; interpolation methods; central, upwind and hybrid formulations and\ncomparison for convection-diffusion problem.",
      "Module 3 Finite Element Methods and Methods of Solution: Finite element methods;\nRayleigh-Ritz, Galerkin and Least square methods; interpolation functions; one- and two-\ndimensional elements; applications.\nSolution of finite difference equations; iterative methods; matrix inversion methods; ADI\nmethod; operator splitting; fast Fourier transform.",
      "Module 4 Time integration Methods and Numerical Grid Generation: Single and\nmultilevel methods; predictor corrector methods; stability analysis; Applications to transient\nconduction and advection diffusion problems.\nNumerical grid generation; basic ideas; transformation and mapping.",
      "Module 5 Navier-Stokes Equations and Turbulence modelling: Explicit and implicit\nmethods; SIMPLE type methods; fractional step methods. Reynolds averaged Navier-Stokes\nequations, RANS modelling, DNS and LES."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Computational Methods for Fluid Dynamics, Ferziger J H and Peric M, 3rd Edition,\nSpringer Verlag, Berlin, 2003",
      "2. Computational Fluid Dynamics, Jiyuan Tu, Guan Yeoh and Chaoqun Liu, Elsevier, 2nd\nedition, 2012\nReference books:",
      "1. An Introduction to Computational Fluid Dynamics: The Finite Volume Method, Versteeg\nH K, Malalasekara W, 2nd Edition (Indian Reprint) Pearson Education, 2008"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. To classify equations into parabolic, elliptic, and hyperbolic types and recognize their\nimportance in fluid flow analysis.",
      "2. To learn the finite difference method and its formulation for solving partial differential\nequations.",
      "3. To understand the principles of finite element methods and their application in solving\nfluid mechanics problems.",
      "4. To study time integration methods for solving transient conduction and advection-\ndiffusion problems.",
      "5. To explore different methods (explicit, implicit, SIMPLE type, fractional step) for\nsolving the Navier-Stokes equations."
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "After completion of this course the student should be able to:"
    ]
  },
