  "CV545": {
    "code": "CV545",
    "name": "Traffic Engineering",
    "branch": "CVE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/CVE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "CVE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Traffic stream characteristics: Road user, vehicle and highway characteristics,\nFundamental parameters and relations of traffic flow, Traffic stream models. Speed data\ncollection and analysis, Density and travel time measurement and analysis, Moving Observer\nMethod, Automated Traffic Measurements - Traffic forecasting and growth studies. Capacity\nand level of services of roads. Pedestrian studies – flow characteristics - Design principles of\npedestrian facilities.",
      "Module 2: Traffic Management: Parking studies – parking statistics, parking surveys, parking\nrequirements - on street and off-street parking. Lay-byes and bus stops. Principles of Traffic\nControl: Basics of traffic management. Traffic Signs, Road Markings. Traffic System\nManagement – speed, vehicle, parking, enforcement regulations. Mixed traffic regulations –\none way, tidal flow, turning restrictions etc.",
      "Module 3: Design of Intersections for Safety: Uncontrolled intersection, Conflicts at\nintersection, Channelization, Traffic islands, Design of median islands, turning vehicle\ntemplates. Traffic intersection control: Traffic Rotaries – design of traffic rotaries. Traffic\nsignal design - Design Principles of Traffic Signal, Coordinated Traffic Signal, Vehicle\nActuated Signals and Area Traffic Control. Design of Grade Separated Intersection - trumpet,\ndiamond, cloverleaf and flyovers.",
      "Module 4: Accident Investigation and Prevention: Characteristics of road accidents, causes\nof accidents: road – driver – vehicle - environment, Significance of accident data, Accident\nrecording and analysis - Crash reporting and collision diagrams - Statistical Interpretation and\nAnalysis of Crash Data. Identification of potential sites for treatment - Safety countermeasures.\nMonitoring and evaluation. Roadway lighting.",
      "Module 5: Road Safety Audit: Overview, stages of road safety audit, audit process, checklists,\nand elements of good road safety audit. Highway safety improvement program - Safety\nEducation, Traffic Law Enforcement. Road Safety Management System. Case studies."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Highway Engineering, Khanna S K, Justo C E G, Veeraragavan A, Nem Chand and Bros,\nRoorkee, 2014.",
      "2. Principles and Practices of Highway Engineering, Kadiyali L R, Lal N B, Khanna\nPublishers, 2008.\nReference books:",
      "1. The Handbook of Road Safety Measures, Elvik R, Hoye A, Vaa T, Sorensen M, Emerald\nGroup Publishing Limited, 2009",
      "2. Relevant IRC codes"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. To understand the fundamentals of traffic stream characteristics",
      "2. To learn the skills of traffic control and management",
      "3. To learn the methods of safe intersection design",
      "4. To learn the importance and methods of accident investigation and prevention",
      "5. To understand the concepts of road safety audit and safety improvement methods"
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "After completion of the course the students will be able to:"
    ]
  },
  "CV546": {
    "code": "CV546",
    "name": "Disaster Management and Mitigation",
    "branch": "CVE",
    "pdfPath": "/syllabi/CVE2025.pdf",
    "pdfName": "CVE2025.pdf",
    "ltp": "3-0-0",
    "credits": 3,
    "modules": [
      "Module 1: Introduction to Disasters, Classification, Causes, Impacts: Concept and\nDefinitions of different terms of disaster. Approaches to understand disaster phenomena\n(natural science, applied, science, progressive and holistic approaches). Parameters of disaster\nrisk levels of disaster as per national guideline.",
      "Module 2: Classification of Hazards (Natural and Manmade): General characteristics and\nProblem areas of different natural and Man-made hazards (e.g. Flood, Erosion, Earthquake,\nLandslide, Lightning, Tropical Cyclone, Drought, Civil Unrest etc.).",
      "Module 3: Disaster Trends (Global, National and Regional): Response time, Frequency\nForewarning, Exposure time of different hazards. Common approaches to study natural and\nmanmade hazards; Vulnerability and Disasters. Differential impacts- in terms of Caste, Class,\nGender, Age, Location, Disability.",
      "Module 4: Disaster Risk Mitigation: Disaster risk assessment (Hazard-Vulnerability-\nCapacity analysis), Hazard mapping and Forecasting. Principles and Aspects of Disaster\nprevention, Disaster mitigation. Preparedness for damage mitigation and coping with disasters.\nCapacity building for disaster/damage mitigation (structural and non-structural measures).\nContingency planning for damage mitigation of different hazards. Relevance of indigenous\nknowledge, appropriate technology and local resources in disaster risk mitigation. Community\nbased disaster risk reduction mechanism. Counter disaster resources and their roles. Selected\nmodels for understanding the causes of disaster and disaster risk mitigation.",
      "Module 5: Environment and Disasters: Environment, Ecosystem and Disasters. Climate\nchange-issues and Concerns. Industrial hazards and Safety measures. Post disaster impact on\nenvironment. Impact of developmental projects on disaster risk. Aspects of environmental\nmanagement for disaster risk reduction. Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA).",
      "Module 6: Planning for Disaster Management: Community; Hazard profile in India.\nDifferent phases of Disaster Management (DM cycle). Relief mechanism (needs assessment,\nrelief administration and distribution, management of relief centers, external support etc.).\nCompensation and Insurance. Planning strategies (state and district DM planning); planning\n\n\nneeds. Disaster Management Act (2005); Disaster Management Policy (2009); organizational\nframework for disaster management in India."
    ],
    "textbooks": [
      "1. Natural Hazards and Disaster Management: Vulnerability and Mitigation, R. B. Singh,\nRawat Publication, New Delhi, 2006",
      "2. Disaster management, Ghosh G K, APH Publishing Corporation, 2011\nReference books:",
      "1. Disaster Management: Text and Case Studies, Murthy D B M, Deep & Deep\nPublications, 2007",
      "2. Encyclopaedia of Disaster management, Goel S L, Deep & Deep Publications, 2006",
      "3. Citizen’s Guide to Disaster Management: How to Save Your Own Life and Help\nothers, Satish M, Macmillan Publishers India, 2006"
    ],
    "objectives": [
      "1. To describe the basic types of hazards and their potential consequences",
      "2. To understand the strengths and weaknesses of disaster management approaches",
      "3. Understand how to react effectively to natural, man-made, and technological threats.",
      "4. Study the hazard mapping and Forecasting",
      "5. Learn about the aspects of environmental management for disaster risk reduction"
    ],
    "outcomes": [
      "On completion of the course the students will be able to"
    ]
  },
